ically in the business of selling  their banking services their rainmakers  they got to bring in the clients  and they have to balance sometimes  honestly sometimes i would say  under severe pressure the interests of  their own credibility what they're  telling investors what they're telling  the public  and the interests of their own  want to lose the banking business i'll  use a blodget to get an example not to  pick on him there are many  less well-known analysts who are in this  situation but  he used to work uh for a firm i believe  called cibc oppenheimer  he made the call on amazon 1998 that it  would go up by two thirds and  partially fueled by the hype surrounding  that call it did it just skyrocketed  through the target  he then got hired by merrill lynch for a  much more prestigious job presumably for  a lot more money  and he replaced the guy who had been at  merrill lynch who had been covering the  same stock amazon who said amazon was  worth  one eighth as much as henry blodgett  believed it was worth  now what does that tell you about  merrill lynch either it tells you that  they decided the old guy was wrong the  new guy was right or maybe  it tells you that merrill lynch was  interested in becoming a serious player  in the dot-com business  and getting the banking business from  some of these high-tech companies  who they hope would speak the same  language as their analyst  so as as the analysts they just hired so  again we come back to the question of  fortune tellers  are fallible uh they are contradictory  they sometimes have conflicting  agendas some journalists do a good job  of highlighting that many do not and  investors  obviously have a need to be cautious and  i think as we go through some of these  hoaxes and scares and episodes of hype  at least some investors are learning  that sometimes painful lessons  the question is whether anyone was made  to answer for the emulax hoax of a  couple weeks ago  well the fbi did arrest a gentleman who  was  the perpetrator or the alleged  perpetrator of the hoax who had written  the bogus press release he was a former  employee of a company called internet  wire  which is like pr newswire and business  wire  it's a new company that puts out press  releases to news organizations like  bloomberg and  cnbc and the street.com and it's  supposed to check these things but he  did it in such a way that it was not  checked  i understand there are already some  lawsuits pending and  you know the sec will continue to  investigate um but i do think that  there's always going to be fraud in a  market that is nothing new  uh what is new i think is the speed with  which these things can be perpetrated  given  the high impact of the real-time  financial journalism outlets  what troubles me more since you can  never completely 100 guard against the  possibility of being hoaxed  is the more subtle uh forms such as when  cnbc says ebay and yahoo are in talks  the stock goes up the talks turn out to  be not very serious or not very real  the stock goes down um you can say the  stock is bouncing around and eventually  finds its level but if you're the guy  who bought the stock when it suddenly  had to run up and suddenly it's down 20  points  you're not too happy that explanation  because you worry about your portfolio  as you should  start a conversation and it'll go up uh  anyone can send a message about a stock  in a chat room and sometimes  people who um post messages in these  chat rooms which are increasingly  influential a lot of people follow them  um they have a reason for wanting a  stock to go down maybe they want to  short the stock they have a reason for  winning stock to go up maybe they  already own a big batch  the thing about these chat rooms they're  very interesting i've checked them out  myself is  you know you got these little people  with anonymous screen names you know you  don't know what you're getting obviously  you want to exercise some caution  corporations interestingly enough some  of them are going after  people who they believe are doing these  what they regard as slanderous postings  on the internet about their company  sometimes suing america online or some  other internet provider to find out the  identity  of the people who are going online in  their view to trash their company  which shows you how seriously the  corporate executives take this  new online world i think we probably  have time for one more  sure can you discuss what you see  in in the future as it relates to media  you know the growth of  internet tv uh more publications as it  relates to the market  and whether uh the the effect of a bull  market and good economy  also relates to the growth of all these  and whether that  interacts in terms of the changes that  you foresee  the question is about the future of the  media uh the  impact of the bull market and what we  might see unfolding in the future  well this is sort of the part of a talk  where i want to say  you know i'm optimistic that things are  going to get better  but that would be wrong the fact is and  i cover  problems in the media in all guises in  my day job at the washington post  as the market speeds up and as the  ability to get information online speeds  up  and as the pace the seemingly breathless  pace of cable television  speeds up and there are more and more  channels and more and more choices and  more and more information  that in some ways is a boon for  investors but it also uh  creates more possibility for havoc for  misinformation for the rumors we've been  talking about  and it's hard for me to see uh  how news organizations which are under  incredible pressure with all this money  at stake  are going to resist the the urge  to get it out there before it's been  fully checked now i think some news  organizations  are slowly waking up to the fact that  their credibility is on the line  and to the extent that they um  are a little more careful sometimes a  little slower perhaps  willing to be beat by six minutes on a  story so that they can make sure the  story is right so they can get more  comments they can tell you more sides of  that story  but that is going to add to their  credibility and that might ultimately be  a better selling point than being the  first to shout into this  giant megaphone now how the financial  media are going to fair  uh if and when the bull market  eventually fades is an interesting  question that  um that a lot of people don't want to  face up to it's a lot of fun to check  your stocks every 20 minutes when the  thing is going up up up  it's a little harder to turn on cnbc  when all those arrows are in red  and when when the market is having a bad  time and obviously the market's been  quite up and down in the last six months  so it is certainly possible that if the  market goes into a slump or a prolonged  period where people are not  making a lot of money uh that's going to  hurt the ratings on television  that's going to hurt the traffic on  these websites and they will  um that in effect their  financial health is tied to some extent  to the the health of the market itself  which is the reason that some people  feel  that some of them kind of cross the line  to becoming market cheerleaders i would  like to see  news organizations which are after all  filled with some pretty smart folks  um figure this problem out exercise more  self-restraint  care more about the investor at home  than about the bragging rights  for getting the story 12 seconds earlier  that turns out to be  half-baked quarter-baked or sometimes  not put in the oven at all  and i think the more we move in that  direction uh the more we will emerge  with a stronger financial media  and that will be a benefit to consumers  who  may have their their hands on the  computer at home as well  i want to thank you all for coming  thanks very much  thank you very much  howard kurtz is the washington post  media reporter and the author of three  previous books  the fortune tellers is published by the  free press a division of simon  schuster for more information on the  book you can visit their website  simon says dot com  you
 

